// Two contracts that pass `moon prove`, and two different reasons that is not
// the same as the code being right.
//
//   abs_unguarded     proves under both proof models. Its pre-condition is
//                     satisfiable and the implementation honours it. What it
//                     shows is that a `proof_require` is an obligation on
//                     *callers* and not a runtime check: nothing stops a
//                     caller passing a value outside the domain, nothing
//                     reports it, and the result is then whatever the code
//                     happens to do.
//
//   abs_diff_unsound  proves under the default model and is false on the
//                     machine. The default prelude gives `Int` the semantics
//                     of a mathematical integer, so a subtraction that
//                     overflows at runtime is, to the solver, simply a
//                     subtraction.
//
// The second is the one that argues for either writing the machine range into
// the contract by hand, as `src/saturating` does, or proving against the
// machine-integer prelude:
//
//     ./tools/scripts/prove.sh --machine-int pitfall
//
// Measured on the pinned toolchain, that leaves `abs_unguarded` proved and
// `abs_diff_unsound` unproved: its two goals hit the time limit rather than
// coming back invalid, which is a weaker result than a counterexample and is
// reported here as what it is.
options(
  "proof-enabled": true,
)
